
                             FAMEBackUpScripts 1.0
                             ---------------------

FAMEBackUpScripts are scripts which will help you to backup important
FAME data files like settings and user datas.

This release includes the following three backup scripts:

FAMEBackUpConfDatas (1.437 bytes)
FAMEBackUpSettings  (1.588 bytes)
FAMEBackUpUserDatas (1.845 bytes)

Those three scripts above are so called slave or child/sub scripts and have
to be called by the following master script:

FAMEBackUpLauncher  (4.341 bytes)

The master script (launcher) generates in FAME:BackUp/ a new subdirectory
which is named like the current date in DD-MMM-YY form and then calls
the child script with exactly this date as argument.

FAMEBackUpLauncher itself will be called with the child script as argument.

Example calls:

FAMEBackUpLauncher FAMEBackUpConfDatas
FAMEBackUpLauncher FAMEBackUpSettings
FAMEBackUpLauncher FAMEBackUpUserDatas

Note: If your extractor tool has removed the script bits you can set them by 
      typing or snaping the following lines in a shell if you are in the 
      directory where the scripts are copied to:

Protect FAMEBackUpLauncher +s
Protect FAMEBackUpConfDatas +s
Protect FAMEBackUpSettings +s
Protect FAMEBackUpUserDatas +s

This is not important for the subscript call, because FAMEBackUpLauncher
does call it by using "Execute", but it's more easy for you especially
the FAMEBackUpLauncher script should be get the script bit set.

The three example child scripts are doing the following:

FAMEBackUpConfDatas - Is backing up your
                      FAME:Settings/Access/Conferences/<whatever>.dat files.

                      This script was my main reason to create the backup
                      scripts, because i tested the new FAME SystemEditor
                      2.0 feature of reordering and reorganisating all
                      changes of your Conferences in their new positions
                      where the access settings will be updated automatically
                      too. I needed a way to backup my current BBS settings
                      which will be changed by testing out the new functions
                      of FAME SystemEditor 2.0 ;)

FAMEBackUpSettings  - Is the more advanced backup script which is backing up
                      all FAME settings not only the access settings like
                      above except the user datas.
                      In particulary it is backing up the following:

                      - FAME:Settings/#? (all settings including subdirectories
                                         and their files)

                      - FAME:Modules/#?.dat (all settings with .dat extension)

                      - FAME:Modules/CoProcessConfig/#? (all settings including
                                                        subdirectories and
                                                        their files)

                      - FAME:ExternEnv/#? (all settings including
                                          subdirectories and their files)

FAMEBackUpUserDatas - Is backing up your user.data and user.keys files.
                      It also will backup all UserCnf.data files of your
                      Conferences if you modify this script to your needs
                      by adding the paths of your Conferences like explained
                      and showed in example.

That's all.

Feel free to modify these scripts to your needs. And feel free to write new
subscripts (childs) and release them to the public if they are not to specific
to your BBS, but don't bet too much on these script thing, because it will
not directly become invalid, but with FAME 2.0 it will exist a new and better
alternative and then the scripts are no longer needed, but ok if you want to
use them also with FAME 2.0.

A request to all coders:

In FAME 2.0 the new feature of time sheduled co-processes the FAMECoProcess
feature exists. A specially for this feature written tool will take over the
complete backup thing and this after setting up once totally automatical.

I have not the time to write such a backup tool yet, so i ask you if there
is someone who is interrested in writing such a FAMECoProc featuring backup
tool. First it can be written without the FAMECoProc feature which can be
added later, because it's a simple combination of FAMECoProc.library calls.
But if someone wants to code this tool directly with the FAMECoProc feature
from the beginning it's also no problem, because i then will handle to the
one who will programm this tool a FAME 2.0 alpha version which completely
works with the FAMECoProc feature and all needed stuff like the new
additional libraries.

For the backup tool it would be great if it's not simply fixed to default
backup action it is currently knowing off. It should have the possibility
to externally enhance the tool via scripts.

This will be very easy. The backup tool first creates the subdirectory
of the current date and then does it's own build in and enabled backup
stuff and then at the end it calls the additional scripts which can be fully
compatible to the three child scripts above, because the tool need to start
for example such a script with the date of the created directory as argument.

The subscript then can do the rest of the special and additional backup stuff.

For easy use it's the best if the backup tool executes a special directory
where all the subscripts are copied too before, by using the FAME.library
function 'FAMEExecuteDir'.

The backup tool itself should internally support by default the backup stuff
the example subscripts above are doing except the ones of the
FAMEBackUpConfDatas script which is too specific. And for all it should be
allowed to be enable and disable them any time by the SysOp.

So if you are interrested in doing this tool then please contact me by writing
an e-mail to strider@trsi.de or better into the FAME mailing list so that
everyone is able to see who is interested in doing this.

You then if i gave the ok will get all stuff and more informations and help
you will need.

If you need to know more informations or just to come in contact with us
you can reach the FAME Development Team at:

